sub:assertion {
<https://w3id.org/delphi/resource/claim/wolfram2002-ch12-54> a <https://w3id.org/delphi/ontology/Claim>;
    dc:description "Since very simple rules such as the rule 110 cellular automaton are universal, computers could be built from many kinds of components, and a simple chemical process could plausibly assemble a rule 110-like computer at the atomic scale, with one or a few atoms per cell.";
    dc:type "prediction";
    <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#label> "Simple universal rules like rule 110 could be built into computers from atoms";
    <https://w3id.org/delphi/ontology/context> "Stephen Wolfram, A New Kind of Science (Champaign, IL: Wolfram Media, 2002), Chapter 12, “The Principle of Computational Equivalence”; section “Implications for Technology” (from p. 840), p. 841. Scope: molecular and atomic-scale computing technology"
}
